Dependency inventory and designated destinations:

| Source / current use | Narrow input or retained boundary | Behavior to preserve |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `BuildBrushInstance` constructor, `TryUpdateBlockId`, `OnBlockPlacedServer`: `Player.InventoryManager` | Existing `IPlayerInventoryManager` | Initialize from the current slot, retain source itemstack behavior, and reread the active slot for updates/dirty marking. |
| `BuildBrushInstance.TryUpdate`: `Player.CurrentBlockSelection` fallback | Explicit `BlockSelection?`, resolved by client caller | Current null means fall back to the player's selection; callers must resolve that before invoking the new contract. A resolved null clears validity/position and returns false. Preserve force/dirty checks and selection cloning. |
| `BuildBrushInstance.MarkDirty` -> scheduled `DoDirtyUpdate` -> parameterless `TryUpdate` | Narrow `Func<BlockSelection?>` supplied at the game boundary for deferred refresh only | Preserve the existing one-millisecond callback registration/unregistration and read the selection when the callback runs, not when it is scheduled. The callback invokes `TryUpdate` with that explicit current value. |
| `BuildBrushInstance.ActivateDimension` / `SpawnEntity`: name and UID | Name and owner UID strings | Keep warning context and entity ownership; do not change activation, subscription, spawn, or destruction order. |
| `TryGetValidSnappedPosition` / `TryUpdatePlacementValidity`: native `CanPlaceBlock` | `IBrushPlacementValidator` below | Keep the actual block, resolved selection, native dispatch, side effects, and invocation order. |
| Both controllers: brush construction and implicit local-player acquisition | Inventory, identity, validator; explicit selections on updates | Server controller no longer needs a player; client controller operations must not reacquire `api.World.Player`. Keep API services still needed for notices/world access. |
| Client system: `Player` accessor, join/equip/slot events, fast/slow ticks, post-placement refresh | Game-facing extraction of live inventory, current selection, effective offhand, and identity | Keep local-only join handling, lazy brush construction, active/dirty guards, 50/150 ms tick registration, and post-placement refresh. |
| Client `GetBrush`, `RotateCursor`, `CycleSnappingMode` parameters | Local brush and cycle direction where the player argument is unused | Review entity-link/input callers before removal; the current `GetBrush` ignores its argument and uses the local player. |
| Client placement/prediction and lava precheck | Retain real player at native placement; game-mode value if precheck logic is extracted | Keep claim/collision/behavior checks, prediction, active-slot dirty marking, creative-mode exception, packet dispatch, and optional ack gate. Do not extract unrelated precheck policy just to eliminate textual references. |
| Server lookup/join/disconnect/inventory events | Client ID, name, inventory, effective offhand at core operations; real players at events | Preserve per-client controllers, subscription closures, activation/equip changes, destruction/disposal. Do not redesign sequence-map cleanup. |
| Server packet handler | Client ID and packet for decisions; optional name for existing logging | Retain actual sender exclusively for transport/sync; decision behavior is specified below. |
| `src/Extensions/Player.cs` hammer detection | Effective `ItemSlot?` | Integration resolves `InventoryManager?.OffhandHotbarSlot ?? Entity.LeftHandItemSlot`; only then inspect the collectible. Preserve short-circuit fallback behavior. |
| Harmony `BlockBuildHammerIntercept`, server/client `CanPlaceBlock` and `DoPlaceBlock` | Retained native-player boundaries | Preserve server-only interception, pass-through guards, block/itemstack/selection/rotation handling and native virtual dispatch. |
| `BuildBrushInstance.SyncDimensionToPlayers` -> `BrushDimension.SyncToPlayers` -> `IMiniDimension.CollectChunksForSending` | Retain `IPlayer[]` integration forwarding | Keep current recipient list and initialized-dimension guard; do not introduce a new sync abstraction. |
| `BuildBrushEntity.TryLinkToBrushInstance`: implicit local UID / player lookup | Retain game entity-link boundary; adapt brush lookup call only | Preserve local-owner filtering, association, and subscriptions. |
| `BuildBrushEntityRenderer.DoRender3DOpaque`: `capi.World.Player.Entity.CameraPos` | Retain renderer camera boundary | No renderer/camera redesign or player mock is needed for the brush refactor. |

Placement-validation contract:
- Public interface `IBrushPlacementValidator`, owned by `src/BuildBrush/Placement/IBrushPlacementValidator.cs`, with `bool CanPlace(Block block, BlockSelection selection, ref string failureCode)`. Use the module's existing namespace conventions; public visibility permits its use by the existing public brush API.
- Internal sealed adapter `PlayerBrushPlacementValidator`, owned by `src/BuildHammer/Placement/PlayerBrushPlacementValidator.cs`, takes `IWorldAccessor world` and the real `IPlayer player`. Keep those private and invoke `block.CanPlaceBlock(world, player, selection, ref failureCode)` without replacing rules, catching native failures, cloning the supplied selection again, or changing side effects.
- Client/server systems create the adapter when constructing their session-owned brush/controller. Its world/player must be the same objects that the current brush would supply; do not resolve different players or allocate adapters per validation call.
- Each existing brush check starts with an empty failure-code string, forwards it by reference, and currently uses only the boolean result. Preserve that behavior; do not add failure-code presentation or change authoritative placement call sites.
- `ResolveFinalSelectionPosition` uses `_blockUntransformed` through `TryUpdate`: check requested snapping first, then `None` only if rejected, even if the positions coincide. No caching/deduplication of native calls. Null-block paths perform no native validation.
- `TryUpdatePlacementValidity` uses `CurrentPlacementBlock` at the existing resolved `Position`. Missing block/position returns false without assigning a new validity value; preserve this distinction rather than silently resetting it.
- `BuildResolvedSelection` clones the original and changes only resolved position / `DidOffset`; keep face, hit, and other selection data intact.
- Native source `vsapi/Common/Collectible/Block/Block.cs:CanPlaceBlock` checks replaceability, intersecting entities, claims, and block behaviors. Claim denial also dirties the active slot. These source observations constrain the adapter; they are not fresh in-game verification of the installed binary.
- Testability design: inject a fake/mock validator that records block/selection, assigns `failureCode`, and returns an ordered true/false sequence. Construct brush/controller fixtures from inventory mocks, real slots, strings and selections. No fake player or null-player call to native validation is required. Compiling this interface and executable tests belongs to Phases 2–3.
- Deferred-selection design: both game-facing systems supply a getter bound to the same session player that the brush currently stores, e.g. `() => player.CurrentBlockSelection`, for `DoDirtyUpdate` only. A test can supply a mutable selection getter without a player. Ordinary ticks and post-placement controller refresh pass explicit selection values; do not remove the dirty callback, snapshot a selection at scheduling time, or make the core acquire `api.World.Player`. This is a narrow value-provider dependency, not another player interface or a lifecycle redesign.

Packet behavior before extraction (`BuildBrushSystem_Server.HandlePacket_SetBuildBrush`):

| Condition | Tracking/state effects | Ack and synchronization |
| --- | --- | --- |
| No controller for sender client ID | Return immediately; no tracking/state writes | No ack; no sync. |
| Controller exists, tracking absent | Effective last sequence is zero | Apply the comparisons below, including rejecting zero/negative incoming sequences. |
| Incoming sequence <= last sequence | Optional ignored-update log; no map/state changes | Ack existing sequence to original sender; return without sync. |
| Incoming sequence > last sequence | Optional accepted-update log; write map first; call `controller.ApplyState(packet)` next | After successful application ack incoming sequence to original sender, then sync `[fromPlayer]` only when `Dimension?.IsInitialized == true`. |

- State application order remains `State.Apply`, then brush active state, snapping, orientation, position, and last-applied sequence; activation and dirty events can occur during those assignments.
- No new exception recovery/transaction semantics: if state application throws, existing earlier map writes remain and later ack/sync is not reached.
- The proposed decision operation stays on the owning server component and uses client ID/packet; a minimal result may distinguish unknown, ignored, and applied updates and carry the ack sequence. The caller keeps the associated controller for the existing post-ack sync. Do not add packet fields, player wrappers, or a generic transport interface.
- Test consequences: `BrushSyncTests` currently mocks `IServerPlayer`, invokes the private handler via reflection, and verifies only an ack call plus unchanged map for an older packet. Rewrite around decisions in Phase 4; add equal/accepted/unknown paths there. Transport recipient identity and actual chunk delivery remain integration evidence.

Affected fixture inventory:
- Shared `BuildBrush/TestHelpers.cs:CreateMockPlayer/CreateTestInstance` supplies a player mock with inventory/current selection. Replace that fixture with narrow inputs and update direct constructor users.
- `BuildBrushInstanceTests.cs`, `BuildBrushInstanceTests_Client.cs`, `BuildBrushInstanceTests_Server.cs`, `BuildBrushDimensionTests.cs`, `BuildBrushPlacementTests.cs`, and `BlockPlacementInterceptTests.cs` create brush instances via that mock/helper; some configure active slots or current selection.
- `Tessellation/TessellationIntegrationTests.cs`, `Tessellation/RotationModeTessellationTests.cs`, and `E2E/BrushToTessellationFlowTests.cs` use the same construction dependency. The E2E harness wires real brush/dimension components with mocked engine services; it does not establish live rendering acceptance.
- `BrushDirtyCounterTests.cs` independently mocks server identity/inventory just to construct the controller; retain dirty-event assertions after narrowing. `BrushSyncTests.cs` has the separate handler dependency described above. These twelve files cover the current player-mock/helper references; keep assertion coverage rather than removing tests.

