### Phase 3 implementation and verification

- Brush inputs -> Phase 1 inventory/identity/selection contract + `BuildBrushInstance` constructor, `TryUpdateBlockId`, `OnBlockPlacedServer`, `DoDirtyUpdate`, `SpawnEntity` -> replaced the stored player with `IPlayerInventoryManager`, owner UID/name values, a validator and a callback-time selection getter. Active slots are reread on refresh/dirty marking. `TryUpdate` now requires an explicit selection; the deferred callback unregisters first and resolves its supplied getter at execution time. Initialization, source-itemstack handling, missing-selection clearing, force/dirty guards and entity validity assignments retain their established behavior.
- Composition/lifecycle -> both controllers + `BuildBrushSystem_Client/Server` + entity linkage -> the client system supplies a lazy brush factory, and the server system constructs its owner's brush before handing it to the server controller. Controllers no longer acquire or accept players; native adapters are created only at the game-facing construction sites. Client lookup/rotation/snapping no longer take redundant player arguments; tick and post-placement callers pass current selections, and entity linkage uses the local lookup without a player parameter. Existing event handling, ownership, controller disposal and native placement/sync boundaries remain intact.
- Equipment -> `src/Extensions/Player.cs` retains the player-facing `TryGetBuildHammer` / `IsHoldingBuildHammer` adapters at game integration sites. They preserve the short-circuit `InventoryManager?.OffhandHotbarSlot ?? Entity.LeftHandItemSlot` resolution and delegate detection to `src/Extensions/BuildHammerSlotExtensions.cs`, which inspects only an effective `ItemSlot?`. The shared player mock helper remains removed; brush/controller tests use narrow inputs.
- Fixtures -> `TestHelpers`, brush/dimension/placement/intercept fixtures, tessellation fixtures and `E2E/BrushToTessellationFlowTests` -> `BrushTestInputs` supplies inventory/rule mocks, a mutable selection and real slots to construct actual brushes. Ordinary component fixtures explicitly default to accepted validation; native rules are isolated behind the production adapter, and the Phase 2 decision tests still cover rejection/fallback. Existing assertions were retained, including rotation itemstack and dirty/tessellation flows. `BrushDirtyCounterTests` constructs a narrow controller. `BrushSyncTests` only changes its controller construction to compile; its actual sender/handler proxy dependency is intentionally left for Phase 4.
- New contract coverage -> `BrushInputContractTests` (seven cases) and `BuildHammerSlotTests` (three cases) -> checks active-slot replacement/dirty marking, initial/current source block, explicit versus absent selection, callback-time selection/unregistration, forced validity refresh, lazy client construction/post-placement update, controller unsubscription, actual preview-entity owner UID, and hammer/non-hammer/empty slots. These are headless component receipts, not live appearance, permissions or multiplayer acceptance.
- Final delegated validation -> source candidate based on `6550a11` plus this working diff; receipts at `C:\Users\Sisco\AppData\Local\Temp\vbe-player-dependencies-phase3-final-20261023-081621` (`build-final.log`, `focused-final.log/.trx`, `full-final.log/.trx`, `summary.json`, installed API/environment details and `candidate-hashes.json`). Using process-local `NUGET_PACKAGES=C:\Users\Sisco\.nuget\packages`, `dotnet build VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --no-incremental` succeeded with zero errors and 28 warnings. `dotnet test` on that project with `--no-build --no-restore --filter "FullyQualifiedName!~BrushSyncTests"` and TRX output passed all 368 migrated tests, zero skipped. The full run without the filter passed 368 of 369, zero skipped; its sole failure remains `BrushSyncTests.Server_Ignores_Older_Seq_Packets` with the known `IServerPlayerProxy.IsInInteractionRangeOf` missing-implementation `TypeLoadException`. No test was removed or skipped to obtain the focused pass.
- Documentation scope -> current architecture module/flow constraints, the historical `8a34ff7:BuildBrushSimplification.todo`, and the active general project checklist were consulted/reconciled as in Phase 1. No unrelated feature work, packet decision extraction, native API change, tool patch, game launch or package qualification was included. Permanent architecture cleanup remains Phase 5; Phase 4 remains responsible for the final packet-handler fixture and full-suite pass.

