PHASE 4 — Separate server packet decisions from player transport

Depends on: Phase 3.
Outcome: Packet sequence and state tests no longer invoke an `IServerPlayer`-dependent handler.

- [x] Extract the existing sequence acceptance and state-application decision from `BuildBrushSystem_Server.HandlePacket_SetBuildBrush` into an owning, narrowly scoped operation.
  - [x] Accept client ID, packet, and only additional values required for the existing decision/logging behavior.
  - [x] Preserve controller lookup, last-applied tracking, older/equal rejection, accepted state application, and the acknowledgment sequence selected for each path.
  - [x] Keep operation policy with its owning server component; avoid a general network abstraction.
- [x] Keep the registered handler as the game-facing boundary.
  - [x] Extract identity from the real `IServerPlayer`, invoke the decision operation, and send the acknowledgment to that same player.
  - [x] Preserve no-controller behavior and the order of acknowledgment versus owner-only initialized-dimension synchronization.
  - [x] Do not move synchronization ahead of state application or perform it for rejected updates.
- [x] Rewrite `BrushSyncTests` around the player-independent operation, removing reflection-based invocation of the player handler.
  - [x] Cover unknown client, older/equal sequence, and accepted sequence with state/tracking and acknowledgment assertions.
  - [x] Retain transport and real-player synchronization as explicit integration acceptance items rather than claiming these unit tests cover them.
- [x] Search all test sources for player mocks and eliminate any remaining fixture dependency.
- [x] Have a subagent run the full unit-test project and investigate every remaining failure without dropping assertions or skipping tests to obtain a pass.

